 <p><span class="lab">Proof.</span> Remove a balanced edge if present, and treat its 2 ends as 2 edges of equal size, which is consistent because \(d-1=\tau-\sum_c\tau_c\) with \(\tau=s-1\) holds at them too. In \(T\) the poset is 2 chains, and a size occurs once in each chain at most. For a size \(z\) occurring once, \(N\) gives its ancestor sizes, each once, so its parent size is as in \(T\). For \(z\) occurring twice, a size occurring twice above it is an ancestor of both copies. Let \(D\) be the lowest such, and let \(A',B'\) be the singly occurring sizes between \(D\) and \(z\) in the 2 chains. \(N\) vanishes between \(A'\) and \(B'\), so they cannot lie above a common copy. 2 sizes in \(A'\) are comparable, so they lie above the same copy, or the upper one would be above both, and \(N\) forbids that. Thus the 2 parent sizes are the lowest of \(A'\) and of \(B'\), or \(D\) when one is empty, as in \(T\). Summing over \(z\) gives the children statement. Now \(d_v-1=\tau_v-\sum_c\tau_c\) on internal vertices, and a vertex with parent size occurring once keeps its degree. A size occurring twice with children \(\xi,\eta\) changes \((\tau-\tau_\xi)^2+(\tau-\tau_\eta)^2\) to \(\tau^2+(\tau-\tau_\xi-\tau_\eta)^2\) when gathered, an increase of \(2\tau_\xi\tau_\eta\). \(\sum d_v\) is fixed. <span class="lab">Exact check:</span> on the 27-vertex pair the formula predicts \(2\cdot5\cdot5+2\cdot1\cdot2=54\), and direct computation gives 54.</p>
