 <p>2 lessons matter for any continuation. Exact finite data was repeatedly more suggestive than reliable: the single-exchange classification held through 19 vertices and failed at 27, and the weight 12 deficit was a cutoff artifact. And the method that worked on caterpillars, feeding the shell 2 relation into shell 3 and reading a top degree, relies on a linear order of cuts. Theorem 38 means general trees need unbounded data, so an extension must be a statement about all shells together; the exchange square and the rooted series are the natural objects for it. The sharpest open points are the sign of Theorem 40, injectivity of \(G\), and Conjecture 46. The Loebl and Sereni reconstruction of caterpillars from the \(U\)-polynomial is a different route, unneeded among caterpillars. The core replay <code>verify_all.sh</code> was rerun on revision 17's package and passed, but it covers the graph pipeline through volume 12 and not the tree results, whose scripts are separate and assume the author's directory layout. All results of this phase need independent review, and no novelty claim is made for them: a literature comparison has not been done.</p>
 
+<p><span class="lab">Recovered supplement, integrated November 5.</span> The full proofs and replay are now included in <code>supplements/caterpillar_proof_advance</code>. For the pendant sequences \((a,b,a-1,a+b,b+1)\) and \((a,a+b,b,a-1,b+1)\), put \(u=q^a\), \(v=q^b\). Its exact volume-4 difference, in the order displayed there, is
+\[\Delta[x^4]M=\frac{uv}{q}(q^2-1)^2(q^2+1)(u-1)(qv-1)(qv-u)F(q,u,v),\]
+\[F=q^4u^2v^2+q^4uv^2+q^4v^2+q^3u^2v+q^3uv+q^3v+q^2u^2+q^2u-q^2v^2+q^2-quv-u^2.\]
+For integers \(a\ge1,b\ge0\), the factor \(F(q,q^a,q^b)\) has unique highest term \(q^{2a+2b+4}\). Thus the difference vanishes identically exactly when \(a=b+1\), the isomorphic case. The finite transfer expansion and the displayed factorization both replay exactly in the recovered supplement. This does not enlarge the scope of general-tree completeness.</p>
